  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the brain combine the senses?",
      "a": "Signals from each sense go first to their own brain areas, then to regions that compare them. The brain links signals that come from the same place at the same time into one event.",
      "asks": [
        "How do senses work together?",
        "What is sensory integration?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do voices seem to come from a ventriloquist's puppet?",
      "a": "Your eyes are better than your ears at telling where something is. When you see the puppet's mouth moving in time with the voice, the brain decides the sound comes from the puppet.",
      "asks": [
        "How does ventriloquism work?",
        "Why does the puppet seem to talk?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the McGurk effect?",
      "a": "If you watch a video of lips saying \"ga\" while hearing \"ba\", many people hear \"da\". The brain merges what it sees and hears into a compromise, showing that sight changes what we hear.",
      "asks": [
        "Can seeing change what you hear?",
        "What is the ba ga illusion?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the brain trust some senses more?",
      "a": "The brain weighs each sense by how reliable it is right now. Vision usually wins for location, but in the dark or fog, hearing and touch count for more.",
      "asks": [
        "Which sense is strongest?",
        "Does the brain prefer sight?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I experience one world instead of separate senses?",
      "a": "The brain constantly binds together signals from the eyes, ears, skin and other senses that happen together, and adjusts for their different arrival times, so you feel one single scene.",
      "asks": [
        "How does everything feel like one picture?",
        "Why don't senses feel separate?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The McGurk effect was described by Harry McGurk and John MacDonald in 1976.",
    "Light reaches the eye almost instantly, while sound takes about 3 seconds to travel 1 kilometre, yet nearby events seem to be seen and heard together."
  ],
